  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A large Nor'easter continues to spin off the \ncoast of the Northern Mid-Atlantic this morning. Well south of \nthe center of the system, the pressure gradient continues to \nrelax, and this should continue to support winds stepping down \ncompared to the past few days. As the winds weaken, and as the \nNor'easter finally weakens and pulls further away from the \nregion, swell energy will continue to decrease. While some \ncoastal communities will continue to feel the effects of the \nrough surf and strong swell, the magnitude of impacts is \nexpected to take a step down today. In light of this, the \nCoastal Flood Warning for Hatteras and Ocracoke Islands has now \nbeen transitioned to a Coastal Flood Advisory. Areas where dunes\nhave been damaged or weakened, some minor flooding may continue\nthrough 1 or 2 more high tides (around 8:00am this morning and \n8:00pm this evening). Additionally, water may be slow to recede \nwhere roads have been flooded over the past few days, even \nwithout additional sound or oceanside water level rises.\n\nOf note, the one area where new, minor coastal flooding may \noccur is around Roanoke Island. Moderate westerly winds are \nexpected to develop along the length of the Albemarle Sound for \nseveral hours today, and this may support elevated waters levels\nin the Croatan and Roanoke Sounds. It's unclear whether the \nwinds will be strong enough for a long enough duration, but the \npotential exists for up to 1 ft of inundation around those \nsounds, and the Northern OBX Coastal Flood Advisory covers this \nrisk.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An upper level ridge is forecast to build over \nthe Southeast U.S. later this week, but guidance has slowed down\nsome on when this occurs. In light of this, the warmest \ntemperatures of the week have trended towards the end of the \nweek (Thursday/Friday). Beneath the ridge, warming low-level \nthicknesses and persistent southerly flow may allow inland highs\nto get back close to 90 degrees (roughly 10 degrees above \nnormal as we head into the first few days of October). The \nhumidity is forecast to steadily return as well, but with \nridging in place, the chance of showers and thunderstorms looks \nto stay low for much of the week.",
